#--: Overview

The declarations live in #~"lib/system/sync.z", each with its own doc
comment; this page is the prose and the worked examples.

The #~"sync" unit is for data that threads really do share. A thread's own
work is moved to it or borrowed by it (see #~"thread"); what several threads
use at once is a #~"sync" value, and each thread holds its own #b:"handle" to
it. An atomic, a #~"Mutex", an #~"RwLock", a #~"Condvar" and a #~"Barrier"
are handles: #~"x.share" makes another handle to the same shared state, each
task owns the one moved into it, and the state lives until the last handle
ends. A thread changes the state through a #b:"borrow" of its own handle --
locking, storing, notifying, waiting -- so a #~".view" of a handle only reads
(#~"load" an atomic, ask a channel's #~"capacity"). A guard keeps the handle it
locked through: a second lock through one handle while the guard lives is
refused, where it would wait for itself forever. A channel is two owned ends
instead, each moved to the thread that uses it.

#--: Atomics

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"AtomicI32", #b:"AtomicI64", #b:"AtomicU32", #b:"AtomicU64" --
    names for #~"Atomic t" over each integer type every target updates in one
    instruction: #~"load", #~"store", #~"exchange", #~"compareExchange",
    #~"fetchAdd" and #~"fetchSub". Arithmetic wraps.]
  [#*: #b:"AtomicBool" -- #~"load", #~"store", #~"exchange",
    #~"compareExchange".]
  [#*: #b:"memoryorder" -- #~"relaxed", #~"acquire", #~"release",
    #~"acqRel", #~"seqCst", as C11 and Rust name them. Every operation takes
    #~"order:", default #~"seqCst" -- the one to reach for; the weaker orders
    are for code that has proved it needs them. A load cannot release and a
    store cannot acquire: either panics.]

#~"exchange" is C11's name for Rust's #~"swap", which is a zerolang
statement. #~"compareExchange" stores #~"new" only if the atomic holds
#~"current", and answers what it held -- so it stored exactly when the answer
is #~"current":

#code language=zerolang name=counter: """
  # Adder -- adds to a counter it shares
  Adder: class {
      # its handle to the counter every Adder shares
      total: sync.AtomicI64
  } as {
      task: thread.Task null
      call: function {:this} is {
          i: 0.i64
          for i < 1000 loop {
              this.total.fetchAdd value: 1
              i = i + 1
          }
      }
  }

  main: function is {
      total: sync.AtomicI64.create value: 0
      {
          a: thread.spawn task: (Adder total: total.share)
          b: thread.spawn task: (Adder total: total.share)
      }
      print "total \{total.load}"
      seen: total.compareExchange current: 2000 new: 0
      print "it held \{seen}, now \{total.load}"
  }
"""

#--: Locks

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"Mutex T" -- owns a value one thread at a time reaches.
    #~"create value:" moves the value in; #~"lock" waits for the lock and
    answers a #b:"MutexGuard", whose #~"value" is the Mutex's value to read
    and write. Destroying the guard unlocks.]
  [#*: #b:"RwLock T" -- owns a value many threads may read at once, or one
    write: #~"read" answers a #b:"ReadGuard" (its #~"value" is a view),
    #~"write" a #b:"WriteGuard" (its #~"value" writes). A writer waits for
    every reader.]

The value is reached only through a guard, so it is never touched unlocked,
and the lock model keeps the rest straight: a guard keeps the handle it locked
through (the handle can be neither dropped, moved nor locked again while one
lives), #~"value" holds the guard for as long as what it answered lives, and a
read guard's value cannot be written. T
is a reference type (#~"AnyRef"); wrap a plain value in a class. A guard
unlocks on the thread that locked, so every guard conforms to
#~"thread.ThreadBound": it never reaches another thread (see
[#> to=./stdlib_thread.html: thread]).

#code language=zerolang name=mutex: """
  # Filler -- appends to a list it shares
  Filler: class {
      # its handle to the list every Filler shares
      m: sync.Mutex (ListVal i64)
  } as {
      task: thread.Task null
      call: function {:this} is {
          i: 0.i64
          for i < 100 loop {
              g: this.m.lock
              g.value.append i
              i = i + 1
          }
      }
  }

  main: function is {
      m: (sync.Mutex (ListVal i64)).create value: ListVal i64
      {
          a: thread.spawn task: (Filler m: m.share)
          b: thread.spawn task: (Filler m: m.share)
      }
      g: m.lock
      print "\{g.value.length} appended"
  }
"""

#--: Waiting

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"Condvar" -- where a thread waits for a condition another makes
    true. Holding a Mutex's guard, the waiter calls #~"g.wait cond: cv":
    it unlocks while it waits and locks again before it returns.
    #~"g.waitTimeout cond: cv ms: 50" gives up after the time and answers
    whether it did. The thread that changes the value under the lock calls
    #~"notifyOne" or #~"notifyAll". A wait may return with nothing notified,
    so the waiter checks its condition in a loop.]
  [#*: #b:"Barrier" -- #~"create count: n"; each #~"wait" returns once
    #~"n" threads have called it, and answers whether this thread is the
    round's leader (exactly one is). The barrier is then ready for the next
    round.]

#code language=zerolang name=condvar: """
  # Slot -- a value and whether it is set
  Slot: class {
      # whether `value` is set
      ready: bool
      # the value
      value: i64
  }

  # Setter -- sets the slot, then wakes the waiter
  Setter: class {
      # its handle to the slot, under its lock
      m: sync.Mutex Slot
      # its handle to where the waiter waits
      cv: sync.Condvar
  } as {
      task: thread.Task null
      call: function {:this} is {
          {
              g: this.m.lock
              with s: g.value do {
                  s.value = 7
                  s.ready = true
              }
          }
          this.cv.notifyAll
      }
  }

  main: function is {
      m: (sync.Mutex Slot).create value: (Slot ready: false value: 0)
      cv: sync.Condvar.create
      h: thread.spawn task: (Setter m: m.share cv: cv.share)
      g: m.lock
      for g.value.ready.not loop g.wait cond: cv
      print "set to \{g.value.value}"
  }
"""

#--: Channels

A channel is two #b:"owned ends", each moved to the thread that uses it.

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"Receiver T" -- the one reading end, which owns the channel.
    #~"(sync.Receiver T).create capacity: n" holds at most #~"n" values
    (#~"0", the default, has no limit). #~"receive" waits for a value and
    answers it as an #~"Option T"; #~"tryReceive" answers #~"none" at once
    when nothing is waiting; #~"for each x: rx" walks the values as they
    come. A Receiver has no copy: a second reader is a second channel.]
  [#*: #b:"Sender T" -- a sending end. #~"rx.sender" makes one and
    #~"tx.copy" another. #~"send value:" moves a value in, waiting while the
    channel is full, and answers whether the Receiver still lives: when it
    does not, the value is destroyed where it was sent.]
  [#*: #b:"ReceiverVal t" / #b:"SenderVal t" -- the same for a value type:
    values are copied in, and #~"receive" answers an #~"optionval t".]
  [#*: #b:"close" -- #~"sync.close sender: tx.take" ends a Sender before its
    scope does.]

#b:"How a channel ends": when the last Sender is gone, #~"receive" drains what
is left and then answers #~"none", and #~"for each" stops. A Sender held by a
task ends when the task does -- as its #~"call" returns -- so a parent that
walks a Receiver stops once its producers have returned. A Sender the walking
thread itself holds would keep the channel open forever: close it first
(#~"sync.close"), or never make one there. When the Receiver ends first, what
it never took is destroyed with it, and every later #~"send" answers
#~"false".

Each value goes to exactly one receiver, in the order sent. A value travels to
another thread, so it may hold no borrow -- #~"send" refuses one, as every
method refuses a lock-holding value (see Ownership, "Locks move with the
value"). A channel of #~"thread.ThreadBound" values never reaches another
thread at all: a task holding either end of it is refused where it is spawned.
Two-way traffic is two
channels: a request may carry a Sender for its own reply, an ordinary owned
field. A channel with a capacity is bounded both ways: two threads each
sending to the other's full channel before either receives wait for each
other forever, so let one side receive before it sends, or leave a capacity
of #~"0".

#code language=zerolang name=channel: """
  # Producer -- sends numbered messages, then ends, and its Sender with it
  Producer: class {
      # where it sends
      tx: sync.Sender String
  } as {
      task: thread.Task null
      call: function {:this} is {
          i: 0.i64
          for i < 3 loop {
              this.tx.send value: "message \{i}"
              i = i + 1
          }
      }
  }

  main: function is {
      rx: (sync.Receiver String).create capacity: 1
      p: thread.spawn task: (Producer tx: rx.sender)
      for each got: rx loop print got
      print "the producer is done: \{rx.receive.none}"
  }
"""

#--: Building

A program that uses #~"sync" links #~"pthread", like one that starts a thread;
every class is written from a C template over the shim
#~"src/runtime/natives/_Z_THREAD_SHIM.inc" (pthreads, or Win32 on Windows).
